A||||4||BroCar 87||no EGR=better accelleration ???||||5||
Z||000000||BroCar 87||08-28-2000||12:16 AM||||Hey everyone, I read somewhere that if I were to remove and plug the hose going from the throttle body to the EGR valve, I would get better accelleration/throttle responce. I also read that there may be an increase in emissions also.<BR>QUESTION TIME:<BR>1. Is is a good idea or bad idea? (opinions apply here)<P>2. Are there any pro's and con's to doing this? (specific)<P>3. What kind of emissions are we looking at here? smoke or just fumes?? <P>Thanks for ANY info.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/NorthFLFieroSIG.jpg">  <BR>87 Coupe "IRON BEAST"<BR>84 SE (project)||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FieroGTRacingInc||08-28-2000||12:21 AM||||Good ????"s<BR>I was wondering about this...<BR>We talked a lil' about it before...<BR> I had asked about running it w/ a carbed setup w/out an EGR..got lil' response though.. maybe we will get some information on this subject...<BR>                         Steve||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||08-28-2000||12:25 AM||||The EGR isn't open during WOT. You might get some part throttle improvement.<P>You will likely get some detonation ar part throttle without increasing the octane some.<P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-28-2000||09:33 AM||||Since EGR is an emmisions device, disabling it or removing it is illegal in the U.S.... That said....<P>disabling it generally won't help performance most of the time. It will likely increase detonation on most motors. The EGR only opens at certain throttle positions on the 2.5, it's ecm controlled on the V6.<P>Emmisions wise I've run exhaust tests with and without it. On the Curb Idle test used here in DE the tested cars passed either way. On dyno emissions tests it may fail. <P>I expect that the majority of people who say it helps performance have bad EGR valves. If the valve is leaking it will screw up performance.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||08-28-2000||10:23 AM||||Disconnecting the EGR will gain some performance. Maybe 1-2 hp but I think the gain is because you will need to advance your timing and get an adjustable fuel regulator. The car will run leaner without it during part throttle. This will cause head temps to rise (more ping on lower octane) Hence the need to re-adjust the fuel pressure for injected engines. EGR isn't used at WOT. From an emission stand point, The EGR eliminates nitrous oxides (NoX). Most states do not test for this on cars previous to 1994. You would need to check your states requirements.<BR>On carb'd engines you need to re-adjust the air/fuel mixture. Basically your 0-60 might decrease by .1/.2 secs if at all. If your really curious, Just plug the vaccum line on topthe EGR advance base timing 2 degrees and test drive it. If you don't feel a difference, set everything back to normal.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 08-28-2000).]||||reg||1
